## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Purpose

This document specifies what AnnotAid v2 must do and the qualities it must have. It is written for three audiences: the people who will approve and host the system, the developers who will build and test it, and the project managers and curators who will use it. It is meant to be read on its own. A reader who has never seen AnnotAid should come away knowing what the system does, who uses it, how they use it, and what constraints govern how it is built.

Requirements are divided into **functional requirements** (Section 3), which describe the behaviour the system must provide, and **non-functional requirements** (Section 4), which describe the qualities that behaviour must have: security, reliability, auditability, capacity and so on. Each requirement is given an identifier, a short title and a narrative explanation of what is required and why.

### 1.2 Product background

AnnotAid is an AI-assisted biocuration tool. *Biocuration* is the work of reading scientific papers and recording specific facts from them, such as sample sizes, p-values, populations studied or genetic variants reported, in a structured form so that they can be compared and analysed across many papers.

Doing this by hand is slow. Handing it entirely to an AI is unreliable. AnnotAid combines the two. A large language model (LLM) reads a paper's PDF and proposes a value for each field the project cares about, together with the passage in the paper that supports it. A human curator then checks each proposal against the source, corrects it where needed and confirms it. The value the AI originally proposed is never overwritten. It is kept alongside the curator's final value, so the project always has an audit trail of what the machine said and what the human decided.

### 1.3 Scope of this release

Version 1 (v1) of AnnotAid already exists. It is a local, single-user application: each person installs it on their own computer, and there are no accounts, no shared data and no roles. Version 2 (v2) turns it into a **hosted web application for one institution**, running several curation projects at once. v2 adds:

- user accounts with login;
- three roles (superadmin, project manager, curator), each with distinct permissions;
- shared projects in which work is assigned to and tracked across several curators;
- background jobs for long-running operations such as bulk paper import and bulk AI extraction;
- deployment, HTTPS and backup on an institutional virtual machine.

Many capabilities already exist in v1 for a single user. They are marked **(v1)** in this document. These are to be *extended* to the multi-user setting, not rebuilt.

This document covers only the "Must have" tier. The "Should have" and "Could have" items (cost estimates, batch cancellation, dashboards, schema locking, DOI duplicate detection, invite links, a global job-queue view) are recorded in `specs.md`. They are outside the scope of this release.

### 1.4 Definitions and abbreviations

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **PMID** | PubMed Identifier. A unique number assigned by the US National Library of Medicine's PubMed database to each indexed paper. |
| **DOI** | Digital Object Identifier. A persistent identifier for a published paper. |
| **PMC** | PubMed Central, NCBI's free full-text archive, from which open-access PDFs can often be retrieved. |
| **NCBI E-utilities** | The National Center for Biotechnology Information's web API, used to verify that a PMID exists and to look up its details. |
| **LLM** | Large language model: the AI that reads the paper and proposes field values. |
| **OpenRouter** | A commercial service giving access to many LLMs through one API and one account key. |
| **Project** | A self-contained curation effort: a set of papers, a schema describing what to extract from them, and the people working on it. |
| **Schema** | The definition of what to extract in a project: the list of fields (called *features*), their types and descriptions, the instructions given to the model, and the model and parse engine to use. |
| **Feature / field** | One item to be extracted from a paper, e.g. "sample size" or "countries studied". A feature may also be a *repeating group*, such as a list of variants each with its own fields. |
| **Parse engine** | The method used to convert the PDF into text the model can read (for example, plain text extraction or OCR). |
| **Extraction** | One run of the LLM over one paper, producing proposed values and supporting evidence for every feature. |
| **Evidence** | The passage from the paper that the model cites in support of a proposed value. It can be highlighted in the PDF viewer. |
| **`aiValue`** | The value the model originally proposed for a field. It is immutable: it is never changed after extraction. |
| **Curated value** | The value the curator has settled on for a field. It starts equal to `aiValue` and may be edited. |
| **Confirm** | The curator's explicit statement that a field's curated value has been checked against the source and is correct. |
| **Pool** | The set of papers in a project that have not been assigned to any curator and are available to be claimed. |
| **Background job** | A long-running task (such as importing 200 papers) that the server performs independently of the user's browser request, reporting progress as it goes. |
| **Superadmin / Project manager / Curator** | The three user roles; see Section 2.3. |
| **(v1)** | Marks a capability that already exists in the single-user v1 and is extended rather than rebuilt. |

