## 3. Functional requirements

Each requirement below keeps the identifier (M1–M23) used in `specs.md`, so the two documents can be cross-referenced. In the text, "the system shall" marks what is mandatory.

### 3.1 Authentication and roles

**M1 — Login, logout and server-side sessions.**
The system shall let a user sign in with their email address and password and sign out again. On successful login, the server shall create a session record and give the browser a cookie identifying it. Every later request is recognised as that user until they log out or the session ends. On logout, the server shall delete the session so the cookie becomes useless even if it was copied. Sessions are held on the server rather than encoded in the cookie so that an administrator can end them. For example, deactivating a user (M18) ends their access immediately instead of when some token expires. A failed login shall not reveal whether it was the email or the password that was wrong.

**M2 — Three roles, enforced on the server for every endpoint.**
The system shall support three roles: superadmin, project manager and curator. Superadmin and project manager are *account-level* roles, attached to the user's account. Curator is a *per-project* role, attached to a user's membership of a specific project. Every request the server receives shall be checked against these roles before any data is read or changed. Hiding a button in the interface is not sufficient: a user who crafts a request by hand must receive the same refusal as one who uses the interface. A request that fails the check shall be rejected without revealing data from the project concerned.

### 3.2 Curator functions

**M3 — Work queue.**
When a curator opens a project, the system shall show them a work queue with two parts. The first, "my papers", lists the papers assigned to them or claimed by them. The second is the unassigned pool, the papers nobody has taken yet. The curator shall be able to filter the queue by paper status (see M9), for example to see only papers still in progress. The queue is the curator's starting point: it answers "what should I work on next?" without them having to consult a separate spreadsheet or task list.

**M4 — Paper view (v1).**
Opening a paper shall show the PDF alongside the extracted fields. For each field the curator can see the value the AI proposed and the evidence it cited. Clicking the evidence shall scroll the PDF to that passage and highlight it, so the claim can be checked against the source at a glance. The fields can be viewed in two modes. **List mode** shows every field at once. **Card mode** shows one field at a time with next/previous controls and automatically highlights that field's evidence. Card mode is intended for careful field-by-field review. This view exists in v1 and shall be carried over to the multi-user setting.

**M5 — Per-field edit and confirm, with the AI value preserved (v1).**
For every field, the curator shall be able to change the value and mark it as confirmed. The AI's original proposal (`aiValue`) shall be stored separately and shall never be changed, whatever edits follow. The interface shall show when the curated value differs from what the AI proposed. This pairing is the core of AnnotAid's audit trail. It records, field by field, where the model was right and where a human corrected it. That record is needed to trust the curated data, and to measure how well different models perform.

**M6 — Select text in the PDF to set a field value (v1).**
The curator shall be able to select text in the PDF viewer and apply it directly as the value of the current field, rather than retyping it. For list-type fields the selection is added as a new entry. For single-value fields it replaces the value. For fields with a fixed set of allowed values, it is matched against that set. This changes only the curated value. The `aiValue` and the AI's cited evidence are left untouched, consistent with M5.

**M7 — Only the assigned curator may edit a paper.**
A paper shall be editable only by the curator it is assigned to, or by the curator who claimed it from the pool. Other curators on the project may be able to see it but shall not be able to change it. This prevents two people unknowingly working on the same paper and overwriting each other's decisions. It also means every paper has one clearly responsible person. The rule shall be enforced on the server (M2). Project managers and superadmins, who can reassign papers (M17), change the responsible curator by reassigning rather than by editing around them.

**M8 — Every edit and confirmation is attributed and time-stamped.**
Whenever a field value is changed or confirmed, the system shall record which user did it and when. In a single-user tool this was unnecessary. With several curators, and papers that can be reassigned, the project needs to be able to answer "who decided this value, and when?" for any field in any paper. This record also supports quality review and resolving disagreements.

**M9 — Paper status lifecycle.**
Each paper shall carry a status describing where it is in the curation process:

- **Unassigned.** The paper is in the project, typically with its AI extraction complete, but nobody is responsible for it yet. It sits in the pool.
- **In progress.** A curator is responsible for the paper, either because a manager assigned it to them (M17) or because they claimed it from the pool (M3). Editing (M5, M6) takes place in this state.
- **Submitted.** The curator has finished reviewing the paper and declared their work on it complete.
- **Excluded** or **Unextractable.** The curator has decided the paper cannot or should not be curated. *Excluded* covers a paper that turns out to be out of scope for the project. *Unextractable* covers a paper that is in scope but from which the required information cannot be obtained (for example, it simply does not report it). In both cases the system shall require the curator to give a written reason, so the decision can be reviewed later.

The normal path is unassigned → in progress → submitted. Excluded and unextractable are the alternative endings. The status shall be visible in the work queue (M3) and usable as a filter. This release does not define a transition back out of submitted, excluded or unextractable (see Section 6).

**M10 — Autosave to the server.**
The curator's work shall be saved to the server continuously as they work, without them having to press a save button. Closing the browser tab, losing the network briefly or switching computers shall not lose confirmed or edited values beyond the last few seconds of activity. Curating one paper can take a long time, and a single lost session would cost more than the tool saves.

### 3.3 Project manager functions

A project manager can do everything a curator can (Section 3.2) within the projects they manage, and additionally:

**M11 — Create projects.**
A project manager shall be able to create a new project. The user who creates it automatically becomes that project's manager. A project is the unit of work in AnnotAid: its own papers, its own schema and its own team. Separate studies are kept apart and can run side by side on the same installation.

**M12 — Schema editor (v1).**
A project manager shall be able to define and edit the project's schema through a visual editor. This covers the list of features (fields) to extract, including their types, descriptions and allowed values, and repeating groups such as a list of variants. It also covers the system prompt (the standing instructions given to the model), the model to use, and the parse engine. The schema decides what the AI is asked for and in what shape the answers must come back, so it is the most consequential setting in a project. The editor shall validate the schema and show a preview of the prompt that will be sent to the model, so the manager can see the effect of their descriptions before paying for an extraction. This editor exists in v1 and shall be carried over.

**M13 — Add curators to the project.**
A project manager shall be able to add existing user accounts to their project as curators, and remove them. Managers do not create accounts themselves; that is a superadmin function (M18). A manager chooses from people who already have an account. This keeps account creation, and therefore control over who can reach the system at all, with the superadmin, while letting managers staff their own projects.

**M14 — Add papers, as a background job (v1).**
A project manager shall be able to bring papers into the project in four ways:

1. a single PMID;
2. a file containing many PMIDs;
3. a single PDF upload;
4. a bulk upload of many PDFs.

For PMIDs, the system shall verify the identifier with NCBI and then try, in turn, several open-access sources (PubMed Central, OpenAlex, Unpaywall, Europe PMC and others) to retrieve the PDF automatically. Each paper in a batch shall appear as its own row with its own progress and outcome: added, already in the project, or failed with a stated reason. Failed rows shall be retryable. When no open-access PDF can be found, the manager shall be offered a *PDF fallback*: they upload the PDF themselves for that specific PMID. Because a large batch can take many minutes, the import shall run as a background job (M21). The manager can then leave the page and return to see its progress, rather than keeping a browser tab open for the duration. The single-user version of this flow exists in v1.

**M15 — Duplicate detection (v1).**
When papers are added, the system shall detect papers already in the project, first by the content of the PDF file and second by PMID. The content check uses a hash, a fingerprint computed from the file's bytes, so the same PDF uploaded twice under different file names is still recognised. A duplicate shall be reported as "already in the project" rather than as an error, and shall not be added a second time. Duplicates are checked within a project: the same paper may deliberately be curated independently in two different projects.

**M16 — Bulk extraction as a background job.**
A project manager shall be able to start AI extraction over many papers at once. The extraction shall run as a background job (M21), because each paper can take a minute or more with the model. The system shall show the status of each paper individually (waiting, running, done or failed). Where a paper fails, it shall show the error, and the manager shall be able to retry that paper without re-running the ones that succeeded.

**M17 — Assign and reassign papers.**
A project manager shall be able to assign each paper to a specific curator, or leave it in the pool for curators to claim (M3). They shall also be able to reassign a paper from one curator to another, for example when someone leaves the project or a workload needs rebalancing. Assignment is how the manager divides the work. Combined with M7, it determines who may edit each paper.

### 3.4 Superadmin functions

A superadmin can do everything a project manager can (Section 3.3), across every project, and additionally:

**M18 — Manage user accounts.**
A superadmin shall be able to create user accounts, set each account's role, reset a user's password manually, and deactivate users and their project memberships. Because the system sends no email (Section 2.5), the superadmin is the only route by which accounts are created and forgotten passwords are replaced. Users are *deactivated* rather than deleted. A deactivated user can no longer log in and their active sessions end, but their name stays attached to the edits and confirmations they made (M8). Deleting the account would break the audit trail.

**M19 — Archive and read every project.**
A superadmin shall be able to open and read any project, whether or not they are a member of it, and shall be able to archive a project. Archiving retires a finished or abandoned project from active use without destroying its data, which remains available for reference and audit. Read access to all projects lets the administrator oversee the system and deal with problems in any project.

**M20 — Assign additional project managers.**
A superadmin shall be able to add further project managers to a project beyond the one who created it. This lets responsibility for a project be shared, or handed over when its original manager is unavailable or leaves.

### 3.5 Platform functions

**M21 — Background job runner.**
The system shall provide a background job runner that executes long operations (bulk paper import, M14; bulk extraction, M16) independently of the user's browser. Every job shall have a visible state: **queued** (waiting to start), **running**, **done**, **failed** or **cancelled**. When a job fails, the error text shall be recorded and shown, so the user knows what went wrong rather than just that something did. The browser shall obtain progress by checking the job's status periodically. As described in Section 2.5, jobs run on a single worker thread inside the one server process.

**M22 — Deployment with Docker and Caddy.**
The system shall be packaged as a single Docker container and deployed on the institutional virtual machine behind Caddy, which provides HTTPS. Deployment and upgrade shall consist of replacing that one container. The database and PDF storage live on persistent volumes, so they survive container replacement.

**M23 — Nightly backup with a tested restore.**
The database file and the PDF volume shall be backed up every night. It shall also be demonstrated, before go-live, that a backup can actually be restored to a working system. A backup that has never been restored is an untested assumption. The restore test is what turns it into a real safeguard.

