## 4. Non-functional requirements

These requirements describe qualities of the system as a whole, not individual features. Each notes the functional requirements or constraints it derives from.

### 4.1 Security

**NFR-1 — Authenticated access only.** Apart from the login page, every page, API endpoint and file shall require a valid session. An anonymous request shall receive nothing but a refusal or a redirect to login. *(M1, M2)*

**NFR-2 — Authorisation checked on the server, on every request.** Role and project-membership checks shall happen on the server for every request, as described in M2 and M7. The browser interface may hide actions a user is not allowed to take, as a convenience. It shall never be the thing that prevents them. *(M2, M7)*

**NFR-3 — Password storage.** Passwords shall be stored only as salted scrypt hashes, never in plain or reversible form. Nobody, including a superadmin or someone holding a copy of the database, shall be able to read a user's password back. *(Section 2.5)*

**NFR-4 — Session protection.** Session cookies shall be httpOnly, so page scripts cannot read them. They shall be marked Secure, so they are sent only over HTTPS. Logout (M1) and deactivation (M18) shall invalidate the server-side session immediately. *(M1, M18)*

**NFR-5 — Encrypted transport.** All traffic between users and the system shall use HTTPS, terminated by Caddy. The application shall not be reachable over plain HTTP except for a redirect to HTTPS. *(M22)*

**NFR-6 — Protection of stored PDFs.** PDFs shall be served only through authenticated endpoints that check the user's access to the owning project. There shall be no public or guessable URL for any stored file. *(Section 2.5)*

**NFR-7 — Secret handling.** The OpenRouter key and any other service credentials shall be held only on the server. They shall never be sent to the browser, written to logs, or included in exports. The per-user, unencrypted key storage of v1 shall not be used in the hosted version. *(Section 2.5)*

**NFR-8 — Limited outbound access.** The server needs outbound network access only to OpenRouter and the named literature services (NCBI E-utilities and NCBI web, PMC and its open-data store, OpenAlex, Unpaywall, Europe PMC). The system shall work under a firewall policy that permits only these destinations.

### 4.2 Auditability and data integrity

**NFR-9 — Immutable AI output.** Once recorded, an `aiValue` shall never be modified by any user action or any later process. The curated value is stored separately. *(M5, M6)*

**NFR-10 — Complete attribution.** Every change to a curated value or confirmation state shall be attributable to a user and a time. That attribution shall survive deactivation of the user concerned. *(M8, M18)*

**NFR-11 — No silent lost updates.** Concurrent activity (two users saving, or a background job writing while a curator saves) shall never cause one save to silently overwrite another. The single-process design (Section 2.5) and the single-editor rule (M7) together provide this. Any change to the deployment that adds server processes must preserve it. *(M7, M10, M21)*

**NFR-12 — No duplicate papers within a project.** A project shall never hold the same PDF content or the same PMID twice. *(M15)*

### 4.3 Reliability and recoverability

**NFR-13 — Durability of curation work.** A curator's edits shall reach the server within seconds of being made (M10). Work shall not depend on the browser tab staying open. *(M10)*

**NFR-14 — Isolation of failures in batches.** In bulk import and bulk extraction, a failure on one paper shall not stop or undo the others. Successful items are kept, and failed items are reported individually with their reason and can be retried. *(M14, M16, M21)*

**NFR-15 — Recoverability.** The system shall be restorable from the most recent nightly backup to a working state, and this shall have been demonstrated (M23). With nightly backups, the most work that can be lost in a total loss of the server is the work done since the previous night's backup. This is accepted for this release. *(M23)*

### 4.4 Performance and capacity

**NFR-16 — Supported scale.** The system shall support about 10–15 registered users with about 2 working at the same time. The architecture (single process, SQLite, single worker thread) was chosen for this scale. A substantial increase in concurrent users shall trigger a review of that architecture rather than simply adding server processes (see NFR-11).

**NFR-17 — Responsiveness under long operations.** Operations that may take longer than a normal page request, such as bulk import and model extraction, shall run as background jobs. They shall not keep the user's request waiting. The interface shall show progress for them rather than appearing frozen. *(M14, M16, M21)*

**NFR-18 — Operational limits.** The system shall accept uploads of up to 64 MB per request, to accommodate large PDFs. It shall allow each model call up to 300 seconds before treating it as failed. The reverse proxy (Caddy) shall be configured with limits at least this generous, so that it does not cut off valid uploads or healthy extractions before the application would.

No numerical response-time or availability targets have been set for this release. If required, they are to be agreed with the hosting provider (see Section 6).

### 4.5 Usability

**NFR-19 — Continuity with v1.** Users of v1 shall find the curation experience unchanged: the same paper view, evidence highlighting, list and card modes, and select-to-set behaviour. v2 adds accounts, queues and assignment around that core without altering it. *(M4, M5, M6)*

**NFR-20 — Visible progress and clear errors.** Every long-running operation shall show its progress. Every failure shall be reported with a specific, human-readable reason, together with a way forward where one exists (retry, or upload the PDF instead). *(M14, M16, M21)*

**NFR-21 — No installation for users.** Users shall need only a web browser. Nothing is installed on their own computer.

### 4.6 Maintainability and portability

**NFR-22 — Minimal dependencies.** The server shall depend only on the Python standard library and `jsonschema`. Introducing any further third-party dependency is a change to this specification. *(Section 2.5)*

**NFR-23 — Reuse of v1.** Capabilities marked (v1) shall be extended in place rather than reimplemented, so that their existing tested behaviour is preserved.

**NFR-24 — Single-artifact deployment.** The complete application shall be deployable as one Docker container plus Caddy. Moving it to another virtual machine shall require only that container, its configuration, and the database and PDF volumes. *(M22)*

